
          nread -- a terminal text reader, derived from GNU nano

Purpose

    Nread is a small and simple text reader for use on the terminal.
    It keeps nano's interface and reader conveniences: syntax highlighting,
    line numbers, softwrapping, multiple buffers, selecting text by holding
    Shift, and searching with regular expressions. Editing, saving,
    search-and-replace, formatting, and command execution are removed
    from the interface. Files are always read-only.

    The project began as a replacement for a personal nless shell script
    that opened files or piped output in nano with --view --softwrap
    --zero. That wrapper still had the limitations of an editor running
    in view-only mode. It also copied piped input to a temporary file
    before opening nano, adding disk I/O and startup delay for large
    inputs. Nread makes this workflow a dedicated reader and reads piped
    input directly into its buffer, without that temporary-file step.

Usage

        nread file.txt
        nread file.txt another.txt
        nread +42 file.txt
        command | nread -

    To use the soft-wrapped, full-screen appearance of the nless script:

        nread --softwrap --zero file.txt
        command | nread --softwrap --zero -

    Use arrow keys and Page Up/Down to navigate. With the default key
    bindings, Ctrl+F searches, Alt+W finds another match, Ctrl+R opens
    another file, and Alt+, / Alt+. switch between files. Ctrl+X closes
    the current file and exits when no files remain. Ctrl+G shows help.
    Configuration can change these bindings. Use --help for all options.

Appearance

    In rough ASCII graphics, this is what nread's screen looks like
    with its title and shortcut bars visible:

   ____________________________________________________________________
  |  nread                         filename                 Read-only  |
   --------------------------------------------------------------------
  | This is the text window, displaying the contents of a 'buffer',    |
  | the contents of the file you are reading.                          |
  |                                                                    |
  | The top row of the screen is the 'title bar'; it shows the         |
  | program version, the name of the file, and its read-only status.   |
  | The two bottom rows display the most important shortcuts; in       |
  | those lines ^ means Ctrl. The third row from the bottom shows      |
  | some feedback message, or gets replaced with a prompt bar when     |
  | you tell nread to do something that requires extra input.          |
  |                                                                    |
   --------------------------------------------------------------------
  |                       [ Some status message ]                      |
  |^G Help        ^R Open File   ^C Location    M-6 Copy                 |
  |^X Exit        ^F Where Is    M-A Set Mark   M-] To Bracket           |
   --------------------------------------------------------------------

    The shortcuts shown depend on terminal width and configuration.
    With --zero, the text fills the terminal and these bars are hidden.

Origin

    Nread is forked from the GNU nano development tree at commit
    9de9f1ee, 38 commits after the 9.3 release. The original authors and
    contributors are listed in AUTHORS and THANKS. The following is
    the background of the upstream nano project.

    The nano project was started in 1999 because of a few "problems"
    with the wonderfully easy-to-use and friendly Pico text editor.

    First and foremost was its license: the Pine suite does not use
    the GPL, and (before using the Apache License) it had unclear
    restrictions on redistribution.  Because of this, Pine and Pico
    were not included in many GNU/Linux distributions.  Furthermore,
    some features (like go-to-line-number or search-and-replace) were
    unavailable for a long time or require a command-line flag.  Yuck.

    Nano aimed to solve these problems by: 1) being truly free software
    by using the GPL, 2) emulating the functionality of Pico as closely
    as is reasonable, and 3) including extra functionality by default.

    Nowadays, nano wants to be a generally useful editor with sensible
    defaults (linewise scrolling, no automatic line breaking).

    The upstream nano editor is an official GNU package. For more
    information on GNU and the Free Software Foundation, see
    https://www.gnu.org/.

License

    Nread's code and documentation are covered by the GPL version 3 or
    (at your option) any later version, except for two functions that
    were copied from busybox which are under a BSD license.  The
    documentation is additionally covered by the GNU Free Documentation
    License version 1.2 or (at your option) any later version.  See the
    files COPYING and COPYING.DOC for the full text of these licenses.

    When in any file of this package a copyright notice mentions a
    year range (such as 1999-2011), it is a shorthand for a list of
    all the years in that interval.

How to compile and install nread

    From a Git checkout, generate the build system first:

        ./autogen.sh

    Then configure, compile, and optionally install:

        ./configure
        make
        make install

    You can also run the built reader directly with ./src/nread.
    Bootstrapping requires Git, autoconf, automake, and autopoint/gettext,
    and downloads the pinned gnulib dependency. See README.hacking for
    the inherited build prerequisites.

    You will need the header files of ncurses installed for ./configure
    to succeed -- get them from libncurses-dev (Debian) or ncurses-devel
    (Fedora) or a similarly named package. Use --prefix with ./configure
    to override the default installation directory of /usr/local. Use
    --sysconfdir to choose the preferred system configuration directory;
    nread also checks /etc for an installed nreadrc or nanorc.

Configuration

    Nread retains nano's nanorc syntax. It looks for nreadrc in the
    configured system directory, then /etc. If neither exists, it looks
    for nanorc in those same locations. It then reads the first user
    configuration found in this order:

        ~/.nreadrc
        $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/nread/nreadrc
        ~/.config/nread/nreadrc
        ~/.nanorc
        $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/nano/nanorc
        ~/.config/nano/nanorc

    Use -I to ignore configuration or -f FILE to read only a specific
    file. Settings and bindings for disabled features are silently
    ignored; ignored bindings leave existing reader shortcuts intact.
    Unknown settings still report errors. Search and cursor-position
    history can be saved when explicitly enabled with -H and -P or
    their configuration settings.

    After building, you may want to copy doc/sample.nanorc to your home
    directory, rename it to .nreadrc, and adjust it to your taste. It is
    an inherited nano example, so editor-only settings have no effect.

Tests

    Run the reader regression checks after building. These require
    Python 3 and pyte:

        python3 tests/reader.py ./src/nread

Web Page

    https://github.com/Soccera1/nread

    Upstream nano: https://nano-editor.org/

Upstream Mailing Lists

    There are three nano-related mailing-lists.

    * <info-nano@gnu.org> is a very low traffic list used to announce
      new nano versions or other important info about the project.

    * <help-nano@gnu.org> is for those seeking to get help without
      wanting to hear about the technical details of its development.

    * <nano-devel@gnu.org> is the list used by the people that make nano
      and a general development discussion list, with moderate traffic.

    To subscribe, send email to <name>-request@gnu.org with a subject
    of "subscribe", where <name> is the list you want to subscribe to.

    The archives of the development and help mailing lists are here:

        https://lists.gnu.org/archive/html/nano-devel/
        https://lists.gnu.org/archive/html/help-nano/

Bug Reports

    For nread bugs, please file a detailed description of the problem
    at https://github.com/Soccera1/nread/issues, including how to
    reproduce it and any relevant configuration.

    For problems reproduced in upstream nano, its issue tracker is
    https://savannah.gnu.org/bugs/?group=nano, and its development
    mailing list is nano-devel@gnu.org.
